\bigskip

Emma owns a busy coffee shop near campus. Every morning she sells three drinks:
\textbf{Espressos, Lattes, and Cappuccinos}. Business has been growing, and she is
trying to decide whether it is time to purchase a second espresso machine. Unfortunately, she forgot to record how many of each drink she sold. However,
she knows exactly how much coffee, milk, and foam the shop used during the
morning rush. She wants to reconstruct the sales using mathematics before making
an expensive business decision.



\textbf{Emma's Records:}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|}
\hline
\textbf{Drink} & \textbf{Coffee (oz)} & \textbf{Milk (oz)} & \textbf{Foam (oz)}\\
\hline
Espresso & 2 & 0 & 0\\
Latte & 2 & 8 & 1\\
Cappuccino & 2 & 4 & 4\\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

At the end of the morning rush, Emma finds that the shop used

\begin{itemize}
    \item 60 oz of coffee
    \item 108 oz of milk
    \item 66 oz of foam
\end{itemize}
\bigskip

Emma estimates that buying a second espresso machine is worthwhile only if she
regularly sells at least \textbf{35 drinks} each morning. Before making the
investment, she wants to determine how many of each drink she actually sold.

\section*{Questions}

\textbf{1) Build the system}

Let $x$=\text{number of Espressos}, $y$=\text{number of Lattes}, $z$=\text{number of Cappuccinos}.


Using the recipe table, write one equation for each ingredient: (Coffee, Milk, Foam)

\bigskip


\textbf{2) Matrix Representation}

Write your system as an augmented matrix.

\bigskip

\textbf{3) Solve the system}

Use Gaussian elimination to determine the values of $x, y, z$. Interpret what each number means in the context of the problem.

\bigskip

\textbf{4) Business Decision}

Emma sold $x+y+z$ drinks this morning. Should she purchase the second espresso machine? Explain your reasoning using
your solution.

\section*{Answer Key}

\textit{1)}
$2x+2y+2z= 60$, 
$8y+4z = 108$, 
$y+4z = 66.$ Let \(x\), \(y\), and \(z\) represent the number of Espressos, Lattes, and Cappuccinos sold. Each equation comes from the total amount of coffee, milk, and foam used.

\bigskip

\textit{2)}
\[
\left[
\begin{array}{ccc|c}
2 & 2 & 2 & 60\\
0 & 8 & 4 & 108\\
0 & 1 & 4 & 66
\end{array}
\right]
\]

The augmented matrix is another way to write the system of equations without the variables.

\bigskip

\textit{3)}
$x = 9, y = 6, z = 15$.
Use Gaussian elimination to find the values of \(x\), \(y\), and \(z\).

\bigskip

\textit{4)} The total number of drinks sold was $x+y+z=9+6+15=30$. Since $30<35,$ Emma should \textbf{not} purchase the second espresso machine at this time.

